<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lepanto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573">Ottoman–Venetian War (1570–1573)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lepanto was a naval battle fought on 7 October 1571 in the Gulf of Patras, off western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, between the fleet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">Don John of Austria</a>, and the Ottoman fleet. The League, whose ships came chiefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Papal States, destroyed most of the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. It was the last great battle between galley fleets, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> soon rebuilt their navy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto.jpg" alt="Battle of Lepanto" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laureys a Castro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laureys_a_Castro_-_The_Battle_of_Lepanto.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Order of Saint Stephen in 1561, a crusading order of knights based at Pisa, whose galleys fought the Ottomans and the Barbary corsairs; Tuscan galleys served in the Christian fleet at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, and in 1607 the knights sacked Bona on the Algerian coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mediterranean, galleys from Spain and its Italian kingdoms fought the Ottomans and Barbary corsairs, and a Holy League fleet under Don John of Austria won the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Spain the kingdom supplied soldiers, money and galleys for the Habsburg wars, including ships at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, and viceroys built chains of coastal watchtowers against Barbary corsairs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Papal galleys under Marcantonio Colonna served at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, and the War of Castro of 1641–1644 showed the limits of papal arms against small Italian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Uluj Ali, a Calabrian convert, became beylerbey in 1568, captured Tunis in 1569 and commanded the Ottoman left wing at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Against the Ottoman Empire it fought a series of wars from 1463, contributed about half the galleys of the Holy League at the <b>Battle of Lepanto</b> in 1571, defended Candia for over twenty years until 1669, and conquered the Morea under Francesco Morosini in 1684–1687.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li></ul></section>
